What are the different ways of creating a thread?  It purely depends upon the programming language u use

The following is in C#
Thread.CurrentThread.Name = "Main thread";
Class1 obj = new Class1();
Thread thread = new Thread(
new ThreadStart(obj.ThreadStart));
thread.Name = "Forked thread";

Now in Java
Java's creators have graciously designed two ways of creating threads: implementing an interface and extending a class. Extending a class is the way Java inherits methods and variables from a parent class. In this case, one can only extend or inherit from a single parent class. This limitation within Java can be overcome by implementing interfaces, which is the most common way to create threads
thread.Start();

import java.lang.*;
public class Counter extends Thread 
{                      
        public void run()                       
        {              
        ....            
        }
}

and

import java.lang.*;
public class Counter implements Runnable
{
        Thread T;                        
        public void run()                       
        {                              
        ....            
        }
}

What is a "bag of words" in c++?  I see you've got a project to do!  The document you're trying to classify should be encoded as a feature vector, with each element in the vector indicating the presence or absence of a word in the document.   So you might have a list of words:

std::vector<std::string> words(3);
words[0] = "Kitty";
words[1] = "Puppy";
words[2] = "Moocow";

Then represent your document as a list of bool:

std::vector<bool> wordbag(3);

If the doc contains the word "Kitty", set wordbag[0] to true.  If the doc contains the word "Moocow", set wordbag[2] to true.  Then you can decide how to classify the document based on your wordbag.

